Well, the way that that would work is,
well, we have one valence electron.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.640
We have one lone pair electron, and
it's making no bonds at the moment.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.590
So this would be a formal charge of zero.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.090
So the last little part is valence
electrons minus lone pairs, and

00:01:30.095 --> 00:01:33.810
then that's gonna be minus the
number of bonds around the atom.

00:01:33.815 --> 00:01:38.550
So it's kind of taking into consideration
all these little parts here.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.930
So we're just counting what we have.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:44.100
And the goal here is when we're looking
at these, just to remember, is that.

00:01:44.775 --> 00:01:46.005
These are not real charges.

00:01:46.215 --> 00:01:49.755
This allows us to figure out what the
correct Lewis structure is, because

00:01:49.755 --> 00:01:55.365
the goal here is to have what I would
consider to be the smallest, um,

00:01:55.725 --> 00:02:00.615
